A||N||10||msweldon||5sp Getrag has difficulty going into 1st and Reverse on occasion, any Remedies?||||1||
Z||000000||msweldon||10-10-2000||04:43 PM||||I'm in the planning stages of a 3.4TDC swap for my 87GT so I'll have my Getrag removed.  <P>Sometimes, with the 5sp, when I'm coming to a stop or just cranked and starting, shifting into 1st requires a "good" bit of force(I don't like) and if I throw the tranny into reverse and back into 1st it seems to seat itself sometimes without the heavy effort.  <P>In addition when I first start-up and I attempt to go into reverse I can't stop it from grinding no matter how much I modulate the gears and clutch.<P>Furthermore, on occasion it will move between gears like butter but other times it seems like I have to pop a cable to get it into gear.<P>I've replaced the cables 1 year ago(professionally) so I know thats not it.<P>What gives?  Do I have the infamous fiero clutch problem(throw-out bearing binding etc..)?  <P>Someone please help.<P>I'd like to know how to solve this shifting problem before I start the swap.<P>many thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||10-10-2000||04:57 PM||||BBBAD GTs car does that too(5-spd). my 4:10(4spd) did that but replaced synchros and bearings and it was fine. not sure about how the 5spds built... hey Jeff you see this!||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||10-10-2000||05:01 PM||||You may have the infamous failing slave cylinder or other hydraulic part. Try bleeding the hydraulic system first and see if that cures your symptoms. If bleeding the hydraulics does cure your problem then replace the slave cylinder. This is a common problem with fiero’s and it happened to mine. The slave cylinders are of a poor design and air gets into them easily when they get old.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Quadfiero||10-10-2000||05:18 PM||||That happened to me before. I did the same thing that 88formula did. I also had so much slop in the linkage that it cost 1.5" of pedal travel. My clutch was never fully disengaged. <BR>     Goodluck||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||10-10-2000||10:14 PM||||Try changing the tranny fluid and adjusting the cables.  My getrag is a bit tough to get into first when I'm at a stop, I've just been to lazy to do anything about it... as for the grinding into reverse, I'm pretty sure that's normal.  There are no synchro's on reverse.  Shift into second or third, then reverse.  That should stop most of the grinding.  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Chance||10-11-2000||06:05 AM||||When I read your post, I could of swore I wrote it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> my 86gt does the same thing, some times it works, sometimes it don't.  I replaced the slave on mine and bled it, but its still not right.  Im going to go through , and bleed it again, replace the tran fluid, and more or less spend a whole day on it.  If I figure something out, I will let you know.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||10-11-2000||07:39 AM||||I double clutch.  Shift to neutral, pump the clutch, shift into gear.  Syncros?  It doesn't happen often enough for getting it fixed for now. Must be a GM thing.  Any GM car I've had with a manual transmission does it.  Especially the reverse thing.||||reg||3
Z||000007||msweldon||10-11-2000||09:01 AM||||Oh yeah...I almost forgot the kicker.  About 2 years ago I was on my morning commute to work trudging through traffic w/ my 5spd when over the course of about 5 minutes my clutch would "not" disengage.  I was having to slam the clutch into the floor repeatedly to change gears.<BR>I looked at my old fiero club newsletters, the old "Matt Gruber" club of the late 80's and early 90's and found the infamous fiero "clutch" problem and remedy.  I discussed the problem with a local mechanic who owns a fiero, as I had nowhere near the facilities in my suburbian garage to drop my tranny, and he read over my files and said no problem and agreed to let me assist.  He'd have it dropped, fixed and reinstalled within 4 hrs.  I decided to go ahead and replace the clutch with a hi-po clutch.  I fandangled a Centerforce Stage II out of a local auto-parts store for the price of a Stage I, "long story".  <BR>Then, on the day I picked up my clutch and was about to limp my fiero 2 miles down to the shop, whaallaaa!!! no problem, easy shifting, hasn't given me any problems, other than those described in my first post of this thread, for 2 years know.<P>Any clues?<P>Just thought I'd fire this one out to see if anyone had any explanations.<P>thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||10-11-2000||09:18 AM||||What change to make it work?  Was this a Fiero miracle repair.  You prayed to the Fiero Gods and YOU ARE HEALED!!||||reg||5
Z||000009||msweldon||10-11-2000||10:26 AM||||Other than letting the car sit for two days while I located a clutch I did nothing to the car, nada, zip, zilch.  <P>The clutch is just now starting to slip after about 75K miles on it.  The car has 137K miles.<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000010||fldevel||10-11-2000||11:33 AM|||| i would start by just checking tranny fluid level ..i remember th time those goof balls at the car shop left my tranny drain plug loose and by the time i got home my car was hard to shift into all gears except 4th so i tightend it up added fluid and it was fine till i noticed a crack going down the side of the tranny case ....im just waiting for a mechanic willing to fix it ..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> dont have the equipment to do it here ||||reg||1
